============================================
               *** README ***
============================================

Brainwaves are rhythmic electrical activity of the brain, measured in cycles
per second (Hz).  The "brainwave entrainment" hypothesis holds that a
rhythmic external stimulus (sound, light or touch) can pull the brain's
activity toward the stimulus frequency.

What is well established: rhythmic sound evokes a measurable brain response
that follows the stimulus, the auditory steady-state response (ASSR), which
is largest near 40 Hz (Chaieb et al. 2015).  What is NOT established:
that this response reliably shifts the brain into a whole-brain "state".
A 2023 systematic review of 14 EEG studies found 5 consistent with
entrainment, 8 against and 1 mixed.  No study found entrainment in the
beta band (Ingendoh et al. 2023).  Behavioural effects of binaural beats are
real but modest: overall g = 0.45 across 22 studies (Garcia-Argibay et al.
2018), and in several studies they appeared without any measurable EEG
entrainment (Engelbregt et al. 2021).

Flashing light is a different matter.  In photosensitive people, flicker
can trigger seizures, most often at roughly 15-20 flashes per second.  That
is a visual effect.  It does not carry over to sound.

40 Hz AND ALZHEIMER'S DISEASE.  From 2016 the MIT Picower Institute (Tsai
lab) showed that 40 Hz light flicker, and later 40 Hz sound (1 ms clicks at
10 kHz, 40 per second), for 1 hour a day reduced amyloid-beta and tau,
prevented neuron loss and improved memory in Alzheimer's mouse models.
Light and sound together worked best (GENUS: Gamma ENtrainment Using
Sensory stimuli).  Pilot human studies found 40 Hz light + sound safe, with
signs of preserved brain volume and cognition, and Cognito Therapeutics has
launched a pivotal trial.  Caution: a 10-day course of 40 Hz light (2 h a
day) produced no change in amyloid in six patients (Ismail et al. 2018).
Human benefit is not yet proven.

THE AUDIO SPECTRUM.  Human hearing spans roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz; the
upper limit falls with age, and many adults hear little above 15-17 kHz.
Sound below 20 Hz is INFRASOUND.  It is not inaudible: it can be heard or
felt at very high levels (about 90-100 dB SPL at 12 Hz), but ordinary
headphones and speakers cannot produce it at those levels.

Extremely Low Frequency (ELF, 3-300 Hz) in the submarine-communication sense
refers to ELECTROMAGNETIC waves.  They are a different physical phenomenon
from sound at the same number of cycles per second.  An audio file cannot
produce them.

The original manual claimed that environments "saturated" with brainwave-
frequency waves shape mood without awareness (alpha environments favouring
cooperation, delta environments making work exhausting).  This comes from
the BWR / MindWar framework.  It has not been tested, and the 1980 MindWar
paper's own source for it is a popular book (Playfair & Hill 1978).

============================================
      BRAINWAVE FREQUENCIES & EFFECTS
============================================

"EEG" lines describe the mental state the band accompanies (a correlation).
"Evidence" lines describe what playing that frequency as sound has actually
been shown to do.

[ 0.5 - 4 Hz ] -> DELTA: DEEP SLEEP
-------------------------------------
- EEG: dominant in deep (N3) sleep; awake delta is not restorative.
- Evidence: delta-range binaural beats for 30 min BEFORE a stressful event
  reduced anxiety (Padmanabhan 2005; Wahbeh 2007b).  Delta beats DURING a
  task worsened vigilance and mood (Lane 1998).  Sleep was not tested.
- Best format: binaural (headphones).

[ 4 - 7 Hz ] -> THETA: DROWSINESS, MEDITATION, RELAXATION
-------------------------------------------------------
- EEG: drowsiness, hypnagogia, meditation; frontal-midline theta during
  internal focus.
- Evidence: reduced pre-operative anxiety after 10 min (Isik 2017).  Theta
  beats IMPAIRED memory encoding (Garcia-Argibay 2017; Wahbeh 2007a).
- Original claim "heightens emotions, frustration; anger suppression":
  not supported; most evidence points the other way.
- Best format: binaural.  Use at rest, not while studying.

[ 7.83 Hz ] -> SCHUMANN RESONANCE
----------------------------------------------
- The Earth-ionosphere cavity's electromagnetic resonance.  An acoustic
  7.83 Hz beat has no physical link to it.
- Evidence: none specific to 7.83 Hz.
- Best format: binaural (behaves as a theta/alpha beat).

[ 8 - 12 Hz ] -> ALPHA: RELAXED WAKEFULNESS
------------------------------------------------------------
- EEG: relaxed, eyes-closed rest.
- Evidence: 10 Hz beats raised alpha power after 5 min in one study (Kim
  2023), not in several others.  Lowered anxiety (Weiland 2011).  9.55 Hz
  for 12 min before a task improved working memory (Kraus & Porubanova 2015).
- Best format: binaural.

[ 13 - 30 Hz ] -> BETA: FOCUS, ACTIVE THINKING
--------------------------------------------------------
- EEG: focused attention, deliberate processing; excess high beta
  accompanies anxiety (a correlation, not a cause).
- Evidence: 16/24 Hz beats during a 30-min vigilance task improved
  performance and mood vs delta/theta beats (Lane 1998).  20 Hz beats
  before and during study improved recall (Garcia-Argibay 2017).  15 Hz
  beats improved self-rated study in adult ADHD (Malandrone 2022).  No EEG
  entrainment found in this band (Ingendoh 2023).
- Best format: binaural.

[ 15 Hz ] and [ 17 Hz ] -> ORIGINAL "HEADACHE / ANXIETY" CLAIMS
-----------------------------
- No study supports them, and the 15-16 Hz studies above contradict them.
  The claims likely stem from photosensitivity to flashing LIGHT.
- "Mono" correction: a raw 15 or 17 Hz sine is infrasonic and inaudible on
  normal equipment.  The speaker-friendly equivalent of a binaural beat is
  a MONAURAL beat (two tones mixed together), e.g. 400 + 415 Hz.

[ 40 Hz ] -> GAMMA: 40 Hz STIMULATION
-----------------------------------------------
- EEG: gamma accompanies perceptual binding and working memory; it is
  reduced in Alzheimer's disease.
- Evidence: amyloid/tau reduction and memory gains in mice (GENUS, 1 h a
  day; see above).  In humans, 40 Hz binaural beats improved attention
  (Colzato 2017; Hommel 2016; Reedijk 2015; Engelbregt 2021), sometimes
  without any gamma EEG increase.
- Best format: depends on the goal.
    Alzheimer's/GENUS: 40 Hz click train (speakers OK).
    Attention studies: binaural beats; MONAURAL beats at the same tones
    INCREASED errors (Engelbregt 2021).  The original "mono or binaural,
    both effective" is therefore not accurate.

[ 40 - 70 Hz ] -> UPPER GAMMA
---------------------------------------------------------------
- Evidence: the 40 Hz findings do not extend upward.  A 57.3 Hz beat had no
  EEG effect (Lopez-Caballero 2017).  The original claims of frontal-lobe
  stimulation and neuroplasticity are not supported.

============================================
            ADDITIONAL NOTES
============================================

- Binaural = headphones (each ear gets one tone; the beat is created in the
  brainstem).  Monaural / isochronic = speakers or headphones (the beat is
  physically in the sound).
- Binaural beats need tones below ~1000 Hz (400 Hz is typical) and are only
  HEARD as beats up to ~30 Hz difference.  40 Hz binaural beats still evoke a
  measurable brain response.
- Timing matters: exposure before, or before and during, a task gave larger
  effects than exposure during it only (Garcia-Argibay 2018).  Longer
  exposure gave larger effects.
- Beats embedded in music were less effective than unmasked beats; white or
  pink noise backgrounds made no significant difference (Garcia-Argibay 2018).

